---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

export const prerender = true;
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="HallowedTales gathers Catholic legend, folk tradition, and the history behind the customs people still keep - told as stories, not as a verification record."
>
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-8 py-16">
    <p class="mb-2 font-display text-sm uppercase tracking-wide text-gold-text">About</p>
    <h1 class="mb-8 font-display text-4xl font-semibold text-ink">The stories behind the faith</h1>

    <div class="space-y-5 font-body text-base leading-relaxed text-ink">
      <p>
        HallowedTales collects the stories that have grown up around the Catholic faith: the legend of a
        saint and a wolf, the custom of leaving shoes by the door on Saint Nicholas Day, the relic
        that pilgrims have traveled to for centuries, the symbol on a church wall that most people
        walk past. They are the kind of stories passed along by parish, family, and tradition.
      </p>

      <h2 class="!mt-10 font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">How the stories are told</h2>
      <p>
        Each entry tells the story as people have told it, then places it in its own history: where it
        first appears, how it changed along the way, and what the historical record does and does not
        support. Tradition is presented as tradition. Nothing here is offered as documented fact unless
        it is one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tone is warm and curious. Stories that can only be told through horror, gore, or fear are
        left out, and so is anything outside the Catholic tradition itself.
      </p>

      <h2 class="!mt-10 font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">What is here</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2">
        <li><a href="/legends" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">Legends</a> - saint-and-creature stories, patronage origins, founding and shrine legends</li>
        <li><a href="/biographies" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">Saint Biographies</a> - whole lives, told plainly</li>
        <li><a href="/traditions" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">Folk Traditions</a> - feast-day customs, food, and the practices handed down in families</li>
        <li><a href="/relics" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">Relics and Legendary Artifacts</a> - the True Cross, the Holy Lance, and the objects whose stories outlasted the evidence</li>
        <li><a href="/symbols" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">Symbols</a> - what they mean and how their use has changed</li>
        <li><a href="/phenomena" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">Mystical Phenomena</a> - the reports that cling to certain saints' lives</li>
        <li><a href="/calendar" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">The Calendar</a> - feast days and liturgical seasons, with the stories that belong to each day</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="!mt-10 font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">A companion to The Miracle Register</h2>
      <p>
        If you want the documented record, with sources and medical review, visit{' '}
        <a href="https://themiracleregister.org" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">The Miracle Register</a>. It holds itself to a strict
        standard of evidence, which is exactly why this site is a separate place: lore cannot meet that
        standard, and it should not have to. Where a story here overlaps a verified case, the entry
        points you across.
      </p>

      <h2 class="!mt-10 font-display text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">About the project</h2>
      <p>
        HallowedTales is an independent project. The source code is public on{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/Labnerd-net/HallowedTales" class="font-medium text-accent hover:underline">GitHub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
